{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Terminal` - what a program may assume about the thing it is printing to.
   ==========================================================================================

   Colour, cursor control, box drawing, aligned tables and a status line that rewrites itself in
   place. One module rather than a facility of whatever happens to need it first, because every one
   of those is the same question asked once - *what is on the other end of this stream?* - and the
   answer is a value.

   **The answer being a value is the whole design.** A `Terminal` is five scalars: how much colour
   the stream takes, whether it draws in Unicode, whether a human is watching it, and how large it
   is. Nothing in this file asks the operating system anything; the one function that does is
   `detectTerminal`, in the platform file beside it. Three things follow, and each of them is why the
   split is here rather than at the printing site:

    - **the fallback is not a code path.** A host with no terminal answers `plainTerminal()`, and
      every function here already handles that value because it is the same function with different
      numbers in it. There is no `if colour is supported` at a call site, ever;
    - **it is testable**, which a terminal library otherwise is not. A test builds the `Terminal` it
      wants to render for and compares the text - see `test/lib/Terminal.yana`, which asserts the
      Unicode and the ASCII rendering of one table against each other;
    - **a caller may lie to it.** `--color always` in a program that pipes its output to a pager is
      the user overriding the detection, and it is a field rather than a switch this module reads.

   **Nothing here writes.** `paint`, `Table.render` and the rest answer strings and the caller prints
   them, which is what keeps the module free of an opinion about *where* output goes. `Status` is the
   one exception and has to be: it exists to overwrite what it wrote last.

   The escape sequences are ECMA-48's, which is what every terminal emulator in use speaks and what
   `TERM=dumb` is the standard way of saying is not there.
-}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Colour
   ==========================================================================================
-}

{-
   How much colour a stream takes, as the three answers that differ in what may be *sent*.

   `NoColour` is not a degraded mode: it is a stream that gets no escape sequences at all, which is
   what a file, a pipe into `grep`, and a terminal whose user set `NO_COLOR` all want. The other two
   differ by eight colours - the bright half is aixterm's 90-97, which every emulator written since
   about 1995 has and a hardware VT does not.

   No 256-colour or 24-bit rung. Those are worth having when a program is choosing colours from a
   gradient; a program that is saying *pass*, *fail* and *skipped* has three colours to choose and
   would use the same three at any depth, so the rung would be a wider type for no different output.
-}
pub data ColorDepth = NoColor | BasicColor | BrightColor

{-
   The sixteen, carrying the SGR number that selects them as a foreground.

   `@value` rather than a table, on `Syscall`'s argument: a set of `let`s is a set of integers that
   happen to be spelled with names, and nothing relates them or says what a position holding one will
   accept. The background code is this plus ten, which is ECMA-48's own arrangement and is why one
   number per colour is enough for both.

   `DefaultColor` is 39 - *revert to whatever this terminal's default is* - and is not the same as
   `White`. A program that paints white text is unreadable on the light themes half the world uses;
   one that leaves the default alone is readable on both, so the default is what everything here
   falls back to and `White` is a colour somebody asked for.
-}
pub data Color
  = @value(39) DefaultColor
  | @value(30) Black
  | @value(31) Red
  | @value(32) Green
  | @value(33) Yellow
  | @value(34) Blue
  | @value(35) Magenta
  | @value(36) Cyan
  | @value(37) White
  | @value(90) Grey
  | @value(91) BrightRed
  | @value(92) BrightGreen
  | @value(93) BrightYellow
  | @value(94) BrightBlue
  | @value(95) BrightMagenta
  | @value(96) BrightCyan
  | @value(97) BrightWhite

{-
   Everything one run of text can be, as a record of scalars.

   A record rather than a list of attributes to apply, because that is what makes a style a *value* a
   program can name once and use in twenty places - `let failed = Style {foreground: Red, bold:
   True}` - and what makes `paint` one escape sequence rather than five nested ones. Every field is a
   scalar, so a `Style` is trivially copyable and costs nothing to pass.
-}
pub data Style {
    foreground: Color = DefaultColor,
    background: Color = DefaultColor,
    bold: Bool = False,
    dim: Bool = False,
    italic: Bool = False,
    underline: Bool = False,
    inverse: Bool = False
}

pub fn plainStyle() -> Style = Style {}
pub fn colored(color: Color) -> Style = Style {foreground: color}
pub fn boldColored(color: Color) -> Style = Style {foreground: color, bold: True}
pub fn dimStyle() -> Style = Style {dim: True}
pub fn boldStyle() -> Style = Style {bold: True}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The terminal itself
   ==========================================================================================

   Five scalars, and `plainTerminal()` is the one every fallback in this module is written against:
   no colour, no Unicode, nobody watching, eighty columns. That is what a redirected stream is, what
   a host with no console is, and what a caller that has not asked gets - so the conservative answer
   is the *default* rather than something a caller has to remember to select.
-}
pub data Terminal {
    depth: ColorDepth = NoColor,
    unicode: Bool = False,
    interactive: Bool = False,
    columns: Size = 80,
    rows: Size = 24
}

pub fn plainTerminal() -> Terminal = Terminal {}

-- Everything on, for a caller that knows better than the detection - `--color always` - and for a
-- test, which is the other thing that needs to name a terminal that is not the one it is running on.
pub fn richTerminal() -> Terminal =
    Terminal {depth: BrightColor, unicode: True, interactive: True, columns: 80, rows: 24}

-- The same stream with the colour taken out, which is what a caller that has decided not to paint
-- wants: the widths, the Unicode and the in-place rewriting are unaffected, because none of them is
-- about colour.
pub fn Terminal.withoutColor(self: Terminal) -> Terminal:
    let &out = self
    out.depth = NoColor
    return out

pub fn Terminal.withoutUnicode(self: Terminal) -> Terminal:
    let &out = self
    out.unicode = False
    return out

pub fn Terminal.quietly(self: Terminal) -> Terminal:
    let &out = self
    out.interactive = False
    return out

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Painting
   ==========================================================================================
-}

-- The SGR number for a colour at a depth. A bright colour on a stream that has only the eight drops
-- to its own base rather than to something nearby, which is the one downgrade that cannot surprise:
-- `BrightRed` becomes `Red` and stays red.
fn colorCode(depth: ColorDepth, color: Color) -> Int:
    let code = truncate(valueOf(color)) :: Int
    if depth == BrightColor || code < 90 then return code
    return code - 60

fn addCode(&codes: String, code: Int) -> {}:
    if length(codes) > (0 :: Size) then codes ++= ";"
    codes ++= "{code}"

{-
   The parameters of one `SGR` sequence, without the escape around them.

   Public because a caller that is building a long line - a table row, a progress bar - can send one
   sequence, write several fields and reset once, where `paint` per field would send two sequences
   per field. Nothing in this module does that yet; the function is what makes it possible without
   anybody re-deriving the numbers.

   `Grey` on a stream with only the eight is the one colour that is *not* downgraded by number: bright
   black is black, which is invisible. It becomes the default colour dimmed, which is what grey is
   for - a foreground somebody is meant to read past.
-}
pub fn Terminal.sequenceFor(self: Terminal, style: Style) -> String:
    let &codes = "" :: String
    if self.depth == NoColor then return codes

    let greyed = self.depth == BasicColor && style.foreground == Grey

    if style.bold then addCode(codes, 1)
    if style.dim || greyed then addCode(codes, 2)
    if style.italic then addCode(codes, 3)
    if style.underline then addCode(codes, 4)
    if style.inverse then addCode(codes, 7)

    if style.foreground != DefaultColor && !greyed then addCode(codes, colorCode(self.depth, style.foreground))
    if style.background != DefaultColor then addCode(codes, colorCode(self.depth, style.background) + 10)

    return codes

{-
   One run of text, styled.

   The whole of the fallback is the two lines at the top: a stream that takes no colour, and a style
   that asked for nothing, both answer the text. So a call site never tests either, and a program
   whose output is being piped emits byte-for-byte what it would have shown a human minus the
   sequences.

   `0m` and not the individual off-codes. Resetting the attributes one at a time is what a nested
   painter needs and this module has no nesting: a painted run is closed before the next one opens,
   which is also what keeps `displayWidth` able to measure by skipping.
-}
pub fn Terminal.paint(self: Terminal, style: Style, text: String) -> String:
    let codes = self.sequenceFor(style)
    if length(codes) == (0 :: Size) || length(text) == (0 :: Size) then return copy(text)
    return "\x1b[{codes}m{text}\x1b[0m"

pub fn Terminal.inColor(self: Terminal, color: Color, text: String) -> String =
    self.paint(colored(color), text)

pub fn Terminal.bolded(self: Terminal, text: String) -> String = self.paint(boldStyle(), text)
pub fn Terminal.dimmed(self: Terminal, text: String) -> String = self.paint(dimStyle(), text)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The cursor, which only an interactive stream has
   ==========================================================================================

   Each of these is the empty string on a stream nobody is watching, and that is the fallback for the
   whole of the in-place rewriting: a run whose output is a file writes its lines and never erases
   one, with no branch anywhere but here.
-}

-- Back to the start of the line and clear it - `\r` then `EL 2`. The pair rather than `EL` alone,
-- because erasing without moving leaves the cursor where it was and the next write starts there.
pub fn Terminal.eraseLine(self: Terminal) -> String:
    if !self.interactive then return ""
    return "\r\x1b[2K"

pub fn Terminal.hideCursor(self: Terminal) -> String:
    if !self.interactive then return ""
    return "\x1b[?25l"

pub fn Terminal.showCursor(self: Terminal) -> String:
    if !self.interactive then return ""
    return "\x1b[?25h"

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   How wide a string is
   ==========================================================================================
-}

{-
   A unit that is a continuation of the one before it.

   Two ranges and one rule: `0x80`-`0xBF` is a UTF-8 continuation byte, which is what a native
   `String` is made of, and `0xDC00`-`0xDFFF` is a UTF-16 low surrogate, which is what a host one is.
   Neither range can occur on the other target - a native unit is a byte and a host unit above
   `0xFF` is not a UTF-8 anything - so this is one function rather than a platform pair, and it is
   the only place in this module that knows the two targets store text differently.
-}
fn continues(unit: Int) -> Bool = (unit >= 128 && unit < 192) || (unit >= 56320 && unit < 57344)

-- One escape sequence, stepped over. `\x1b[` … up to a byte in `@`-`~`, which is CSI's own framing;
-- anything else after the escape is a two-unit sequence. Returns the position after it.
fn afterEscape(text: String, from: Size) -> Size:
    let count = length(text)
    let &i = from
    if i >= count then return i
    if text.unit(i) != 91 then return i + 1

    i = i + 1
    while i < count && text.unit(i) < 64:
        i = i + 1

    if i < count then i = i + 1
    return i

{-
   How many columns a string occupies, which is not how many units it is made of.

   Two things are subtracted from the unit count: the escape sequences, which occupy none, and the
   continuation units, which belong to a character already counted. That is what makes a *painted*
   cell align in a table with an unpainted one, and it is the reason every alignment function here
   measures with this rather than with `length`.

   **A double-width character counts as one**, which is a real limitation and is stated rather than
   hidden. Getting it right needs the East Asian Width tables, which are a data file larger than this
   whole module; what it costs is that a table of CJK text is under-padded. Every other alignment -
   ASCII, accented Latin, box drawing, the arrows and marks below - is exact.
-}
pub fn displayWidth(text: String) -> Size:
    let count = length(text)
    let &wide = 0 :: Size
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count:
        let unit = text.unit(i)
        i = i + 1

        if unit == 27:
            i = afterEscape(text, i)
        else if !continues(unit):
            wide = wide + 1

    return wide

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Padding, repetition and clipping
   ==========================================================================================
-}

-- `count` copies of `unit`. Sized once, which is what makes a rule across an eighty-column terminal
-- one allocation rather than eighty.
pub fn repeatText(unit: String, count: Size) -> String:
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(length(unit) * count)
    for _ in 0 .. count:
        out.push(unit)

    return out

pub fn spaces(count: Size) -> String = repeatText(" ", count)

-- Left-aligned, right-aligned and centred in a field. Text already wider than the field is answered
-- unchanged rather than cut: a table that silently lost the end of a name would be worse than one
-- whose column came out wide, and the caller that wants the cut asks for it below.
pub fn padRight(text: String, width: Size) -> String:
    let have = displayWidth(text)
    if have >= width then return copy(text)

    let fill = spaces(width - have)
    return "{text}{fill}"

pub fn padLeft(text: String, width: Size) -> String:
    let have = displayWidth(text)
    if have >= width then return copy(text)

    let fill = spaces(width - have)
    return "{fill}{text}"

pub fn padCenter(text: String, width: Size) -> String:
    let have = displayWidth(text)
    if have >= width then return copy(text)

    let before = spaces((width - have) / 2)
    let after = spaces(width - have - ((width - have) / 2))
    return "{before}{text}{after}"

{-
   The text, cut to `width` columns with `mark` on the end of what was cut.

   Walked a character at a time rather than a unit at a time, which is the whole reason this is not
   `String.slice`: cutting between the two halves of a code point produces a byte sequence no terminal
   can render, and cutting inside an escape sequence leaves the rest of the line - and often the rest
   of the screen - painted. So an escape is copied whole and counted as nothing, and a character's
   continuation units come with it.

   A reset is appended when something was cut out of styled text, for the same reason: whatever
   sequence was open at the cut is closed here rather than left running into the next line.
-}
pub fn clipTo(text: String, width: Size, mark: String) -> String:
    if displayWidth(text) <= width then return copy(text)

    let markWidth = displayWidth(mark)
    if width <= markWidth then return String.slice(mark, 0, length(mark))

    let count = length(text)
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(count)
    let &shown = 0 :: Size
    let &styled = False
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count && shown < width - markWidth:
        let start = i
        let unit = text.unit(i)
        i = i + 1

        if unit == 27:
            i = afterEscape(text, i)
            styled = True
        else:
            while i < count && continues(text.unit(i)):
                i = i + 1

            shown = shown + 1

        out.push(String.slice(text, start, i))

    out.push(mark)
    if styled then out.push("\x1b[0m")
    return out

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Glyphs, and the ASCII half of every one of them
   ==========================================================================================

   The characters this module draws with, as a record chosen once per terminal rather than as a test
   at each place that draws. That is what makes the ASCII fallback total: `Table.render` never asks
   whether the terminal has Unicode, it asks the glyphs for a corner, and a terminal that has no
   Unicode hands back `+`.

   The ASCII set keeps every mark one column wide, so a table drawn either way has the same column
   widths and the same shape - which is what makes the two comparable in a test, and what stops a
   fallback from being a different layout as well as a different alphabet.
-}
pub data Glyphs {
    horizontal: String,
    vertical: String,
    topLeft: String,
    topJoin: String,
    topRight: String,
    leftJoin: String,
    cross: String,
    rightJoin: String,
    bottomLeft: String,
    bottomJoin: String,
    bottomRight: String,
    pass: String,
    fail: String,
    skip: String,
    bullet: String,
    arrow: String,
    barFull: String,
    barEmpty: String,
    ellipsis: String
}

pub fn unicodeGlyphs() -> Glyphs = Glyphs {
    horizontal: "─", vertical: "│",
    topLeft: "┌", topJoin: "┬", topRight: "┐",
    leftJoin: "├", cross: "┼", rightJoin: "┤",
    bottomLeft: "└", bottomJoin: "┴", bottomRight: "┘",
    pass: "✓", fail: "✗", skip: "○", bullet: "•", arrow: "→",
    barFull: "█", barEmpty: "░", ellipsis: "…"
}

pub fn asciiGlyphs() -> Glyphs = Glyphs {
    horizontal: "-", vertical: "|",
    topLeft: "+", topJoin: "+", topRight: "+",
    leftJoin: "+", cross: "+", rightJoin: "+",
    bottomLeft: "+", bottomJoin: "+", bottomRight: "+",
    pass: "+", fail: "x", skip: "o", bullet: "*", arrow: ">",
    barFull: "#", barEmpty: ".", ellipsis: "~"
}

pub fn Terminal.glyphs(self: Terminal) -> Glyphs:
    if self.unicode then return unicodeGlyphs()
    return asciiGlyphs()

-- The text, cut to fit with this terminal's own mark on it.
pub fn Terminal.clip(self: Terminal, text: String, width: Size) -> String:
    let g = self.glyphs()
    return clipTo(text, width, g.ellipsis)

{-
   One frame of a spinner, by tick.

   The frames are one string and a unit count rather than an array, because an array of ten literals
   is ten allocations per call at the one place in a program that is called most often. The Unicode
   set is the braille wheel every progress display in use has settled on; the ASCII one is the bar
   spinner that predates it.
-}
fn frameOf(frames: String, units: Size, index: Size, count: Size) -> String:
    let at = (index % count) * units
    return String.slice(frames, at, at + units)

pub fn Terminal.spinner(self: Terminal, tick: Size) -> String:
    if self.unicode then return frameOf("⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠇⠏", 3, tick, 10)
    return frameOf("|/-\\", 1, tick, 4)

{-
   A bar `width` columns wide, `done` of `total` filled.

   Rounded down, so a bar is full only when the work is - a display that shows a complete bar while
   something is still running is the one thing a progress bar must not do.
-}
pub fn Terminal.bar(self: Terminal, done: Size, total: Size, width: Size) -> String:
    if total == (0 :: Size) || width == (0 :: Size) then return ""

    let g = self.glyphs()
    let &filled = (done * width) / total
    if filled > width then filled = width

    let head = repeatText(g.barFull, filled)
    let tail = repeatText(g.barEmpty, width - filled)
    return "{head}{tail}"

-- A rule across the terminal, or across `width` columns of it.
pub fn Terminal.rule(self: Terminal, width: Size) -> String:
    let g = self.glyphs()
    return repeatText(g.horizontal, width)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   Tables
   ==========================================================================================

   A table is a terminal, a row of headings, and a flat run of cells - `length(columns)` of them to a
   row. Flat rather than a run of rows, because the whole of what a caller does with one is append a
   cell at a time in the order it computed them, and a row is then a fact about position rather than
   a container somebody has to build and hand over. A last row with fewer cells than columns is
   padded when it is drawn, so a table built by a loop that stopped early still renders.

   **A cell is stored painted, which is why the table holds a `Terminal`.** `addStyled` asks the
   terminal for the escape sequence at the moment the cell is added, and what the table keeps is a
   string. Two things follow. The alignment is unaffected, because `displayWidth` skips the
   sequences; and a cell needs no type of its own, so there is one less record here than a table
   usually has and no question about what a cell knows.

   Three frames, and which one to use is a question about what surrounds the table rather than about
   what is in it. `BoxFrame` is what a result somebody is meant to read as a unit wants; `RuleFrame`
   - a line under the headings and nothing else - is what a long list wants, because a box around
   forty rows is forty rows of vertical bars nobody reads; `BareFrame` is columns and spaces, for a
   table that is being pasted somewhere else.
-}
pub data Align = AlignLeft | AlignRight | AlignCenter
pub data Frame = BoxFrame | RuleFrame | BareFrame

pub data Column {title: String, align: Align}

pub data Table {
    terminal: Terminal,
    columns: [Column],
    cells: [String],
    frame: Frame,
    headerStyle: Style,
    indent: Size
}

-- An empty table for a terminal. The headings come from `addColumn` rather than from here, because a
-- caller that builds them in a loop and one that writes four calls want the same function.
pub fn newTable(terminal: Terminal, frame: Frame = RuleFrame) -> Table =
    Table {terminal: terminal, columns: [], cells: [], frame: frame,
           headerStyle: boldStyle(), indent: 0}

pub fn Table.addColumn(&self: Table, ->title: String, align: Align = AlignLeft) -> {}:
    push(self.columns, Column {title: title, align: align})

{-
   One cell, appended where the next one goes.

   `add` for the plain case and `addStyled` for the one that carries colour, which is the only
   difference a cell can have from another. The style is applied here rather than kept, so a cell is
   a string from this point on - see the note at the top of the section.
-}
pub fn Table.add(&self: Table, ->text: String) -> {}:
    push(self.cells, text)

pub fn Table.addStyled(&self: Table, ->text: String, style: Style) -> {}:
    push(self.cells, self.terminal.paint(style, text))

pub fn Table.rowCount(self: Table) -> Size:
    let wide = length(self.columns)
    if wide == (0 :: Size) then return 0
    return (length(self.cells) + wide - 1) / wide

-- How wide each column has to be: the widest of its heading and every cell under it, measured in
-- columns rather than in units so that a painted cell is measured by what it shows.
fn columnWidths(table: Table) -> [Size]:
    let wide = length(table.columns)
    let &widths = [] :: [Size]

    for column in items(table.columns):
        push(widths, displayWidth(column.title))

    for i in 0 .. length(table.cells):
        let at = i % wide
        let have = displayWidth(table.cells[i])
        if have > widths[at] then widths[at] = have

    return widths

fn alignIn(text: String, width: Size, align: Align) -> String:
    match align:
        AlignLeft -> return padRight(text, width)
        AlignRight -> return padLeft(text, width)
        AlignCenter -> return padCenter(text, width)

-- One horizontal rule of a box, with the three characters that differ between the top, the middle
-- and the bottom handed in. Every column is drawn one wider on each side than its content, which is
-- the padding a boxed cell has.
fn boxRule(g: Glyphs, widths: [Size], indent: Size, left: String, join: String, right: String) -> String:
    let &out = spaces(indent)
    out.push(left)

    for c in 0 .. length(widths):
        out.push(repeatText(g.horizontal, widths[c] + 2))
        if c + 1 < length(widths) then out.push(join)

    out.push(right)
    out.push("\n")
    return out

{-
   One row, from a run of cells starting at `from`.

   **An unboxed row is trimmed at the end**, and that is not a nicety: there is nothing beyond the
   last column to align against, so its padding is trailing whitespace - which a terminal shows as
   nothing, an editor shows as a diff, and `git` shows as an error. It matters most for the row a
   half-built table ends with, where the trailing cells are missing rather than short. A boxed row
   keeps its padding, because there the right-hand border is what the padding is against.
-}
fn Table.renderRow(self: Table, widths: [Size], cells: [String], from: Size, g: Glyphs) -> String:
    let wide = length(self.columns)
    let boxed = self.frame == BoxFrame

    let &separator = "  " :: String
    if boxed then separator = " {g.vertical} "

    let &out = spaces(self.indent)
    if boxed then out.push("{g.vertical} ")

    for c in 0 .. wide:
        let at = from + c

        let &shown = "" :: String
        if at < length(cells) then shown = copy(cells[at])

        out.push(alignIn(shown, widths[c], self.columns[c].align))
        if c + 1 < wide then out.push(separator)

    if boxed:
        out.push(" {g.vertical}")
        out.push("\n")
        return out

    let &line = trimEnd(out)
    line.push("\n")
    return line

-- The headings, painted, as a row of cells like any other.
fn headerRow(table: Table) -> [String]:
    let &cells = [] :: [String]
    for column in items(table.columns):
        push(cells, table.terminal.paint(table.headerStyle, column.title))

    return cells

{-
   The whole table, as the text to print.

   A table with no columns renders as nothing rather than as an empty box, which is what a caller
   that built one conditionally wants. A table with columns and no rows still renders its headings:
   *these are the things that would have been here* is a report, and an empty box is one too.
-}
pub fn Table.render(self: Table) -> String:
    let wide = length(self.columns)
    if wide == (0 :: Size) then return ""

    let g = self.terminal.glyphs()
    let widths = columnWidths(self)
    let &out = "" :: String

    if self.frame == BoxFrame:
        out.push(boxRule(g, widths, self.indent, g.topLeft, g.topJoin, g.topRight))

    out.push(self.renderRow(widths, headerRow(self), 0, g))

    if self.frame == BoxFrame:
        out.push(boxRule(g, widths, self.indent, g.leftJoin, g.cross, g.rightJoin))
    else if self.frame == RuleFrame:
        -- A rule under the headings and nothing else, spanning exactly what the columns and their
        -- separators occupy - so it ends where the last column does rather than at the margin.
        let &span = 0 :: Size
        for c in 0 .. wide:
            span = span + widths[c]
            if c + 1 < wide then span = span + 2

        let line = repeatText(g.horizontal, span)
        let margin = spaces(self.indent)
        out.push("{margin}{self.terminal.dimmed(line)}\n")

    for row in 0 .. self.rowCount():
        out.push(self.renderRow(widths, self.cells, row * wide, g))

    if self.frame == BoxFrame:
        out.push(boxRule(g, widths, self.indent, g.bottomLeft, g.bottomJoin, g.bottomRight))

    return out

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   A line that rewrites itself
   ==========================================================================================

   The one thing in this module that writes, because it is the one thing that cannot be a string: it
   exists to overwrite what it wrote last, and what it wrote last is a fact about the stream rather
   than about the caller.

   **On a stream nobody is watching it does nothing at all** - not "prints a line each time", which
   is what a progress display redirected to a file usually turns into. A build log with one line per
   percent is the failure mode this is written against, and the check is one field.

   The line is clipped to the terminal's width, and that is not cosmetic: a line that wraps has two
   rows on the screen and `\r` returns to the start of the second, so the erase misses the first and
   the display leaves a trail behind it.
-}
pub data Status {terminal: Terminal, showing: Bool, ticks: Size}

pub fn statusOn(terminal: Terminal) -> Status = Status {terminal: terminal, showing: False, ticks: 0}

-- Whether anything this hands it will be seen. A caller that would have to *compute* the line asks
-- this first; one that has it already does not have to.
pub fn Status.visible(self: Status) -> Bool = self.terminal.interactive

pub fn Status.update(&self: Status, text: String) -> {}:
    if !self.terminal.interactive then return

    self.ticks = self.ticks + 1

    -- One column short of the width, because a line that exactly fills the last column wraps on
    -- terminals that place the cursor before scrolling and does not on those that scroll first.
    let &room = 1 :: Size
    if self.terminal.columns > (1 :: Size) then room = self.terminal.columns - 1

    let line = self.terminal.clip(text, room)
    let erase = self.terminal.eraseLine()
    print("{erase}{line}")

    self.showing = True

-- What the next spinner frame is, which the caller puts in the line it hands to `update`.
pub fn Status.spinner(self: Status) -> String = self.terminal.spinner(self.ticks)

-- The line taken back off the screen, so that whatever is printed next starts at the margin. A
-- status that was never shown clears nothing, which is what makes this safe to call unconditionally
-- at the end of a run.
pub fn Status.clear(&self: Status) -> {}:
    if !self.showing then return

    let erase = self.terminal.eraseLine()
    print("{erase}")
    self.showing = False
